\[
\xymatrix{
	d \ar[r]^{w} \ar[d]^{z}  & p(c') \ar[d]^{p(f)} \\
	p(c'') \ar[r]^{p(g)} & p(c),
} 
\]	
where $f:c'\to c$ and $g:c''\to c$ are two arrows of $S$, there is a $K$-covering family $\{d_{i}\to d \mid i\in I\}$ such that for each $i\in I$, the composites $d_{i}\to p(c')$ and $d_{i}\to p(c'')$ belong to the same connected component of the category $(d_{i}\downarrow D^{p}_{S})$. Our $K$-covering family on $d$ will be given by the sieve $\chi^{\ast}(\langle p(P_{S})\rangle )$, where $P_{S}$ is the presieve consisting of the cartesian arrows in $S$ and $\chi$ is the arrow $p(f)\circ w=p(g)\circ z$ witnessing the commutativity of the above square. Notice that, by Theorem \ref{thmfibration1}, this sieve is indeed $K$-covering. We have to check that, for any $\alpha\in \chi^{\ast}(\langle p(P_{S})\rangle )$, the arrows $w\circ \alpha:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(c')$ and $z\circ \alpha:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(c'')$, regarded as objects of the category $({\rm dom}(\alpha)\downarrow D^{p}_{S})$, belong to the same connected component. Since $\alpha\in \chi^{\ast}(\langle p(P_{S})\rangle )$, there is a cartesian arrow $\xi$ in $S$ and an arrow $\gamma$ such that $\chi\circ \alpha =p(\xi)\circ \gamma$. The arrow $\xi$ will actually serve as a means for connecting our two arrows; indeed, we will show that, on the one hand, $\gamma:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p({\rm dom}(\xi))$ and $w\circ \alpha:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(c')$ belong to the same connected component of $({\rm dom}(\alpha)\downarrow D^{p}_{S})$ and that, on the other hand, $\gamma:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p({\rm dom}(\xi))$ and $z\circ \alpha:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(c'')$ belong to the same connected component of $({\rm dom}(\alpha)\downarrow D^{p}_{S})$, from which it follows by transitivity that $w\circ \alpha:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(c')$ and $z\circ \alpha:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(c'')$ belong to the same connected component, as desired. 

To prove that $\gamma:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p({\rm dom}(\xi))$ and $w\circ \alpha:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(c')$ belong to the same connected component of $({\rm dom}(\alpha)\downarrow D^{p}_{S})$, we observe that, since $p$ is a fibration, there are an object $\tilde{c'}$ of $\cal C$, an isomorphism $\tau:{\rm dom}(\alpha)\to p(\tilde{c'})$ and an arrow $h:\tilde{c'}\to c'$ such that $p(h)\circ \tau=w\circ \alpha$. Next, we notice that, since $\chi=p(f)\circ w$ and $\chi\circ \alpha =p(\xi)\circ \gamma$, we have $p(f)\circ w\circ \alpha=p(\xi)\circ \gamma$ and hence $p(f)\circ p(h)\circ \tau=p(\xi)\circ \gamma$, equivalently, $p(f\circ h)=p(\xi)\circ \gamma \circ \tau^{-1}$. From the fact that $\xi$ is cartesian, it thus follows that there is an arrow $\psi:\tilde{c'}\to {\rm dom}(\xi)$ such that $p(\psi)=\gamma\circ \tau^{-1}$ and $\xi\circ \psi=f\circ h$. We thus see from the following diagram that $\gamma$ and $w\circ \alpha$ belong to the same component of $({\rm dom}(\alpha)\downarrow D^{p}_{S})$ (where the vertical unnamed arrow is $p(f\circ h)=p(\xi\circ \psi)$):

